WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.702
FastAPI dependency injection can be a
powerful security tool because it lets teams

2
00:00:07.782 --> 00:00:14.263
express reusable checks and attach them
consistently to path operations.

3
00:00:14.343 --> 00:00:19.693
Dependencies can load the current subject,
validate a token,

4
00:00:19.773 --> 00:00:26.768
require a permission, check a scope, or
prepare request context for later logic.

5
00:00:26.848 --> 00:00:30.346
The value is not the mechanism by itself.

6
00:00:30.426 --> 00:00:37.422
The value is making security enforcement
visible, centralized enough to reuse,

7
00:00:37.502 --> 00:00:41.309
and close enough to the route to
understand.

8
00:00:41.389 --> 00:00:48.076
Role-based checks, permission checks,
scopes, and claims can all be useful,

9
00:00:48.156 --> 00:00:52.580
but they are rarely the whole
authorization story.

10
00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.672
APIs often need object-level
authorization.

11
00:00:56.752 --> 00:01:03.748
The service must verify that the current
subject can access the specific record,

12
00:01:03.828 --> 00:01:09.897
file, tenant, organization, project, or
operation being requested.

13
00:01:09.977 --> 00:01:16.870
A user with a general role may still be
denied for another tenant's resource or an

14
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.757
action outside the current workflow state.

15
00:01:20.837 --> 00:01:28.038
Administrative endpoints and internal APIs
deserve the same discipline as public

16
00:01:28.118 --> 00:01:29.147
endpoints.

17
00:01:29.227 --> 00:01:33.034
Internal does not automatically mean safe,

18
00:01:33.114 --> 00:01:38.875
and hidden documentation or obscure URLs
are not access controls.

19
00:01:38.955 --> 00:01:44.922
If an endpoint can change configuration,
trigger jobs, export data,

20
00:01:45.002 --> 00:01:48.397
impersonate users, or manage tenants,

21
00:01:48.477 --> 00:01:54.135
the server should verify the current
subject, action, resource,

22
00:01:54.215 --> 00:01:56.273
and context every time.

23
00:01:56.353 --> 00:02:00.571
Authorization should be enforced on the
server,

24
00:02:00.651 --> 00:02:04.663
not inferred from which client called the
API.

25
00:02:04.743 --> 00:02:07.624
Good authorization is testable.

26
00:02:07.704 --> 00:02:13.054
Teams should include negative tests for
denied permissions,

27
00:02:13.134 --> 00:02:19.409
cross-tenant access, missing scopes, stale
tokens, object ownership,

28
00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:25.045
administrative actions, and
service-to-service boundaries.

29
00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:31.915
These are defensive tests that prove the
service refuses actions it should not

30
00:02:31.995 --> 00:02:32.612
allow.

31
00:02:32.692 --> 00:02:38.453
When authorization dependencies are
consistent and well tested,

32
00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:43.780
FastAPI path operations are much easier to
maintain safely.
